<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>ProtoLang.LanguageServer</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>protolang-server</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No LSP framework, deliberately. What a framework supplies is transport and routing: Content-Length
    framing, JSON-RPC correlation, a writer lock, and the message shapes this server uses. What it does
    not supply is any of what #42 actually had to get right (the token legend, the diagnostic mapping,
    the configuration pull, the staleness rule), so the choice was between owning three hundred lines
    of transport and owning a dependency graph as well.

    The graph is the part that does not fit here. OmniSharp.Extensions.LanguageServer last shipped in
    2023 and brings MediatR, System.Reactive and the Microsoft.Extensions.* tree onto a net10.0 project
    built with TreatWarningsAsErrors, where one NU warning on a transitive package fails the build.
    This repository has five package references and every one of them is Google or Microsoft
    first-party. Roslyn, rust-analyzer and gopls all own this layer for the same reason.

    It is contained rather than pervasive: everything below Protocol/JsonRpcConnection.cs is transport,
    and nothing above it knows how a message is framed. Swapping in StreamJsonRpc, whose
    HeaderDelimitedMessageHandler already implements LSP framing, would touch that one file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\ProtoLang.Core\ProtoLang.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
